{"metadata": {"file_suffix": "pdf", "producer": {"name": "mineru", "version": "4.0.10"}, "document": {"created_at": "2026-10-03T06:48:36Z", "modified_at": "2026-10-03T06:48:36Z", "creator_application": "TeX", "producer_application": "pdfTeX-1.40.29", "page_count": 2, "page_count_kind": "physical"}}, "extensions": {"mineru": {"tier": "standard", "parse_mode": "txt"}, "docvortex_layout": {"version": 1, "pages": [{"page_idx": 0, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 1, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}]}}, "pages": [{"page_idx": 0, "blocks": [{"type": "header", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.114, 0.044, 0.201, 0.06], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Writing #1"}]}, {"type": "header", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.784, 0.044, 0.885, 0.06], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Anant Gupta"}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.431, 0.144, 0.566, 0.17], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Writing #1"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.111, 0.201, 0.886, 0.295], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "In the Google DeepMind episode Introducing Gemini Robotics 2, the host asks the robotics team if anyone can claim general intelligence without a body. One of the researchers responds, “You cannot reach AGI until you solve physical AGI” [1]. I believe we will find that physical intelligence is the limiting factor on the road to AGI, and that this means the hardest problem is acting reliably where humans cannot jump in to assist."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.111, 0.302, 0.888, 0.521], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Prior to this discussion, a researcher states that their estimates for general-purpose robots entering the world have shrunk from “probably beyond my lifetime” (three years ago), to “maybe ten years” (two years ago) and now “between five to ten years” [1]. The host asks if general intelligence can be claimed without “this embodied characteristic” [1], and the response is that a robot asked to “do anything that I could do” should be able to do it, and that physical AGI “will land after the digital AGI thing has happened” [1]. I would note that the researchers do not belittle language models; they are saying the digital half of intelligence is coming first, and the physical half is the bigger challenge. This problem is called Moravec’s paradox: “things that are really easy for humans are very difficult for robots” [1]. An AI has “passed the bar exam,” but “cannot cook you eggs, or flip a burger” [1]."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.111, 0.528, 0.888, 0.698], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "In the context of this course, this observation is the distinction between planning in a clean symbolic state space and perceiving and learning in a noisy continuous state space. This is because manipulation is “very contact-rich,” requiring a hand with “over twenty degrees of freedom” to coordinate [1]. In an earlier segment, another speaker notes the reason the gap is a challenge: while a human teleoperator can rescue a small robot, “as the robots get bigger, get more capable, gain more degrees of freedom . . . it’s harder and harder for somebody to jump in and help” [1]. Teleoperation data is the most accurate but least scalable form (see Figure 1), so robots inevitably have to learn from their mistakes."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.111, 0.704, 0.888, 0.9], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The video makes no mention of Mars, so this is an extrapolation on my part. A Mars rover is the ultimate expression of the teleoperation problem: since it takes three to twenty-two minutes for a signal to travel between the planets, no one on the ground can “jump in and help” [1] in real time. If physical AGI is five to ten years away, I would offer a delayed intervention test: give a robot a multi-step task (such as cooking a simple recipe), add a twenty-minute delay to any human correction, and observe how often the robot can correct itself. A robot that passes this test would be able to perform the tasks described by the researchers at Google DeepMind. In my opinion, such a robot would be a more convincing step towards proving the capacity for AGI (and ultimately ASI) than another examination of a language model."}]}]}, {"page_idx": 1, "blocks": [{"type": "image", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.266, 0.091, 0.731, 0.199], "content": [{"type": "image_body", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.266, 0.091, 0.731, 0.199], "content": ""}, {"type": "image_caption", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.124, 0.211, 0.875, 0.258], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Figure 1: The robot-training data sources described in the video [1], from most scalable but least precise (bottom) to most precise but least scalable (top). The data-pyramid framing is adapted from NVIDIA’s GR00T N1 report [2]."}]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.112, 0.284, 0.885, 0.375], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The video’s thesis is that intelligence is not merely what a system can say, but what it can do. Moravec’s paradox implies that the last leg of the journey towards AGI will be made not in the realm of language, but of motion, and I believe we will find that systems that can do things without requiring a human safety net will be the ones to arrive first."}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.114, 0.412, 0.244, 0.431], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "References"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "ref_text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.116, 0.453, 0.882, 0.494], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "[1] Google for Developers. Introducing Gemini Robotics 2. URL: https://youtu.be/-rYFDefcq3k?si=V_8kt7WwdQnjHR0w."}]}, {"type": "ref_text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.116, 0.512, 0.882, 0.554], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "[2] NVIDIA. GR00T N1: An open foundation model for generalist humanoid robots. arXiv preprint arXiv:2503.14734, 2025. URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.14734."}]}, {"type": "page_number", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.494, 0.936, 0.506, 0.949], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "2"}]}]}], "is_full_document": true, "schema": "docvortex.middle", "schema_version": "2.0"}